Subject: [PATCH] x86-64: refuse the TLS relocations that presume an FS base

Spike 1 measured a user-written FS base failing to survive a context switch on
this Windows, so DR-0003 moved the thread pointer to a runtime-owned word
reached through %gs. That was taken as a codegen change; it is not. ld relaxes
the psABI's TLS access sequences in place and writes mov %fs:0x0,%rax itself,
out of bfd rather than out of anything the compiler emitted, which
spike/ld-tls-relaxation/ measures.

Refused rather than rewritten. The psABI sizes general dynamic at sixteen
bytes so the two-instruction local-exec form fits in place; the %gs carrier
needs three instructions, so there is no substitution to make. A link error is
the honest answer for an input this target cannot translate, and it makes
hand-written assembly and stray vendor objects visible instead of silently
wrong.

Placed ahead of elf_x86_64_tls_transition, which is load-bearing and was found
by measurement rather than by reading. The transition rewrites r_type in
place, so a check downstream of it inspects the relaxation's output: general
dynamic arrives as GOTTPOFF and is reported under the wrong name, and local
dynamic arrives as an accepted form and goes on to emit the %fs fetch. A first
version of this patch sat after the transition and let tls-ld.s link and leak.

TPOFF32, TPOFF64, DTPMOD64 and DTPOFF64 stay accepted. Those are values rather
than sequences, ld writes no instruction bytes for them, and WP-13's codegen
wants TPOFF for sequences of its own.

Against binutils 2.42, pinned in ../binutils.pin. The target definition these
values answer to is doc/target-definition.md.

--- a/bfd/elf64-x86-64.c
+++ b/bfd/elf64-x86-64.c
@@ -2151,6 +2151,61 @@
 				       symtab_hdr, &no_dynreloc))
 	return false;
 
+      /* This target has no thread pointer in the FS segment base.  Spike 1
+	 measured a user-written base failing to survive a context switch on
+	 this Windows, and spike/ld-tls-relaxation/ caught ld writing the
+	 fetch itself while relaxing these sequences -- out of bfd, rather
+	 than out of anything the compiler emitted.
+
+	 So they are refused rather than rewritten.  The psABI sizes the
+	 general-dynamic sequence at sixteen bytes so that a two-instruction
+	 local-exec form fits in place, and the %gs carrier DR-0003 settled
+	 on needs three, which leaves no substitution to make.  A link error
+	 is the honest answer for an input this target cannot translate, and
+	 it is what makes hand-written assembly and stray vendor objects
+	 visible instead of silently wrong.
+
+	 This runs before elf_x86_64_tls_transition rather than after it.
+	 The transition rewrites r_type in place, so a check downstream of
+	 it sees the relaxation's output: general dynamic arrives as
+	 GOTTPOFF, which is refused anyway but reported under the wrong
+	 name, and local dynamic arrives as a form on the accepted list and
+	 goes on to emit the %fs fetch this exists to stop.
+
+	 TPOFF32, TPOFF64, DTPMOD64 and DTPOFF64 are deliberately absent
+	 from the list.  Those are values rather than sequences, the linker
+	 writes no instruction bytes for them, and the compiler wants TPOFF
+	 for sequences of its own.  */
+      switch (r_type)
+	{
+	case R_X86_64_TLSGD:
+	case R_X86_64_TLSLD:
+	case R_X86_64_GOTTPOFF:
+	case R_X86_64_CODE_4_GOTTPOFF:
+	case R_X86_64_GOTPC32_TLSDESC:
+	case R_X86_64_CODE_4_GOTPC32_TLSDESC:
+	case R_X86_64_TLSDESC_CALL:
+	  {
+	    reloc_howto_type *howto = elf_x86_64_rtype_to_howto (abfd, r_type);
+	    const char *tls_name = (h != NULL
+				    ? h->root.root.string
+				    : bfd_elf_sym_name (abfd, symtab_hdr, isym,
+							NULL));
+
+	    /* xgettext:c-format */
+	    _bfd_error_handler
+	      (_("%pB: relocation %s against `%s' is not supported by this"
+		 " target: it presumes a thread pointer in the FS segment"
+		 " base"),
+	       abfd, howto ? howto->name : "<unknown>", tls_name);
+	    bfd_set_error (bfd_error_bad_value);
+	    return false;
+	  }
+
+	default:
+	  break;
+	}
+
       if (! elf_x86_64_tls_transition (info, abfd, sec, contents,
 				       symtab_hdr, sym_hashes,
 				       &r_type, GOT_UNKNOWN,
